# Bintrail Docker Compose configuration.
# Everything is optional — `docker compose up -d` works with no .env at all
# (add servers from the console UI). Copy to .env to pin any of the knobs.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   docker compose up -d
#   docker compose logs bintrail    # open the printed console URL

# OPTIONAL — a source MySQL to start watching immediately at boot.
# Leave unset and add servers from the console UI instead (zero-config path).
# The user needs REPLICATION SLAVE, REPLICATION CLIENT, and SELECT.
# Baselines additionally need RELOAD (or FLUSH_TABLES), plus BACKUP_ADMIN on
# MySQL/Percona 8.0+, because the snapshot is point-consistent by default,
# and SHOW VIEW so the dump can copy views.
# On managed MySQL (RDS, Aurora, Cloud SQL) BACKUP_ADMIN CANNOT be granted:
# grant LOCK TABLES, SHOW VIEW instead. The console's snapshots notice on
# their own: an Amazon RDS or Aurora host name gets lock-all, which is equally
# point-consistent, and a snapshot whose default (ftwrl) is refused is retried
# once with lock-all. Nothing to set.
#
# BASELINE_LOCK_MODE: only to OVERRIDE that choice. How the baseline dump syncs
# mydumper's threads onto one instant: ftwrl is point-consistent and needs
# RELOAD (+ BACKUP_ADMIN on MySQL 8.0+); lock-all is also point-consistent and
# needs only LOCK TABLES; safe-no-lock needs no extra
# privilege but ABORTS rather than write a snapshot stitched from several
# instants, so expect it to refuse on a write-active source; no-lock accepts
# such a snapshot. A baseline is the seed state reconstruct merges deltas onto,
# which is why the weaker modes must be asked for by name. A value set here
# turns the automatic choice off for every server. The optional one-shot
# `baseline` profile is not automatic: it uses this value, or ftwrl.
#BASELINE_LOCK_MODE=lock-all

# SQL_MAX_IN_FLIGHT: how many SQL statements on the copy (the console's SQL
# card and the time-travel port together) run at once. Empty = 2, right for a
# 4-core host; each takes 2 threads and up to 2 GB beside capture.
#SQL_MAX_IN_FLIGHT=4
# `host.docker.internal` reaches a MySQL on this machine from inside Docker.
# SOURCE_DSN=user:password@tcp(host.docker.internal:3306)/

# Optional — comma-separated schemas to track (empty = all user schemas).
SCHEMAS=

# Optional — an opt-in static token for API automation (scripts/curl against
# the console API). Humans don't need it: on first run the console asks you to
# create a username and password in the browser. Leave empty unless a script
# needs the API.
CONSOLE_TOKEN=

# Optional — container-side bind for the watch daemon's Prometheus /metrics
# endpoint (per-source stream metrics + bintrail_index_* gauges;
# docs/deployment.md §7 has scrape config and alerting rules). Defaults to
# :9090, which docker-compose.yml publishes on the host loopback as
# 127.0.0.1:9090. Set EMPTY (uncomment the line below as-is) to disable.
# METRICS_ADDR=

# Optional — pin the bundled index MySQL root password. Leave commented to
# get a randomly-generated one (the default). If you set it, do so BEFORE the
# first `up` — it is baked into the datadir at init and changing it later
# means resetting the index volumes.
# INDEX_MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=

# Optional — bring your own index MySQL instead of the bundled container
# (then you can delete the index-mysql service from docker-compose.yml).
# INDEX_DSN=user:pass@tcp(my-index-host:3306)/bintrail_index

# Optional — pin the image tag (default: latest). Tags of
# ghcr.io/dbtrail/bintrail-console; the console image exists from the first
# release after the console split onward.
# BINTRAIL_TAG=v0.9.0

# OPTIONAL. Where host.docker.internal points from inside the containers
# (a database on this same machine). Default: host-gateway, which Docker
# Desktop and Docker Engine 20.10+ running as root understand. Rootless
# Docker maps it to an address that is not this machine, and Podman before
# 4.7 refuses the stack with "invalid IP address in add-host": set this
# machine's address here instead.
# HOST_GATEWAY=192.168.1.10
